 "narrative": "The validation responses decisively strengthen the dataset-specific conclusion set. Estimates are signed to the claim direction, so positive signed values support the stated claim and negative signed values indicate the opposite raw association. For SMARCA4 loss, H3 is accepted: the independent validation R13 gives a signed +0.835 composite-dependency-unit stronger-dependency contrast, with lower bound +0.743, well above the 0.15-unit threshold. The new H14 result shows that this SMARCA4 association is not an artifact of TP53-loss co-occurrence: in TP53-wildtype samples the signed estimate is +0.784, lower bound +0.698, again far above threshold. Therefore SMARCA4 loss is supported as a large dataset-specific association with stronger composite dependency, i.e. more negative dependency_composite.\n\nFor TP53 loss, H13 remains supported as a large dataset-specific association with weaker composite dependency. Discovery R1 and independent validation R26 are both signed positive, approximately +0.829 and +0.843, with validation lower bound +0.747. The opposite original H1 claim remains rejected because the same evidence shows the raw contrast in the opposite direction.\n\nFor KEAP1 loss, H38 is now strongly supported and closed. The discovery signed estimate was +0.810 and the independent validation signed estimate is +0.778, lower bound +0.685. Both are far above the 0.15-unit threshold in the claimed positive direction, meaning KEAP1 loss is associated with higher dependency_composite, i.e. weaker dependency, in this dataset. The previously accepted robustness restrictions for KEAP1 remain consistent: matched omics/CRISPR-QC, Avana-library, non-Avana, high-screen-quality, and TP53-wildtype subsets all showed large positive signed contrasts. H46 rejected a meaningful positive KEAP1-by-TP53 interaction: signed +0.077, interval -0.042 to +0.196. The original H22 KEAP1 stronger-dependency claim remains rejected and closed because validation further contradicts it.\n\nSupported findings at this stage are therefore the three large dataset-specific associations: SMARCA4 loss -> stronger dependency, TP53 loss -> weaker dependency, and KEAP1 loss -> weaker dependency. These are validated within the supplied dataset and broadly robust across screen-quality, library, data-completeness, and simple co-occurrence restrictions. They do not establish causality, biological mechanism, or external generalizability.\n\nPTEN loss remains the main unresolved or uncertain moderate signal. The overall H24 estimate is signed +0.239 for stronger dependency, but its lower bound is just below threshold. Conditional estimates are mostly supportive: TP53-wildtype H40 +0.259 with lower bound above threshold, Avana H42 +0.251 with lower bound just above threshold, and matched QC H41 +0.242 with lower bound slightly below threshold. Thus PTEN should be treated as a plausible but threshold-boundary-dependent dataset-specific association rather than a fully mature validated claim. Negative or unsupported follow-up includes H58, the specific negative PTEN-by-TP53 interaction: signed -0.099, interval -0.302 to +0.105. That does not support a meaningful negative interaction, although it does not rule out smaller context effects.\n\nNegative or null closed evidence includes earlier tests of STK11, KRAS, MYC amplification, ARID1A, MTAP, RB1, research markers, and initial lineage/signature interactions. Unresolved areas include CDKN2A borderline effects, PTEN subgroup context, co-interaction tests for SMARCA4/TP53, screen-quality interactions for the large predictors, KEAP1 context-dependence, PTEN context-dependence, and broad mutation/loss markers. The most informative next analysis is to complete the new PTEN conditional means and the KEAP1 TP53-loss mean, and to test H30/H31 plus screen-quality interaction H62/H63. If PTEN conditional means remain consistently above threshold, independent validation or stricter robustness should be considered; if they are mixed, PTEN should remain an exploratory moderate association.",
 "research_notes": "Accepted and closed dataset-specific validated associations: H3 SMARCA4 loss -> stronger dependency (R3,R13); H14 SMARCA4 loss stronger in TP53-wildtype (R14,R40); H13 TP53 loss -> weaker dependency (R1,R26). KEAP1: H38 accepted closed, KEAP1 loss -> weaker dependency (R20,R39). Robustness accepted/closed or supportive: H39 TP53-wt, H43 matched QC, H44 Avana, H45 high screen quality, H53 non-Avana (small exposed cell n=40 but consistent). Main robustness for SMARCA4/TP53: H28 high screen quality SMARCA4, H29 high screen quality TP53, closed. Rejected/closed: H1 original TP53 stronger claim; H22 original KEAP1 stronger claim; H46 KEAP1 positive TP53 interaction; H58 PTEN negative TP53 interaction; earlier negatives include STK11, KRAS, MYC, ARID1A, MTAP, RB1, markers, lineage/signature interactions. Active/unresolved: H24 PTEN moderate stronger-dependency association is accepted but threshold-borderline; H40 TP53-wt PTEN +0.259 lower above threshold; H41 matched QC +0.242 lower slightly below; H42 Avana +0.251 lower just above. CDKN2A H4/H20 remain borderline unresolved. Remaining active context/effect-modification claims: H18/H19 adenocarcinoma means; H30/H31 SMARCA4-TP53 interactions; H32-H37/H48-H52 broad markers; H47/H59-H61 PTEN context; H54-H57 KEAP1 interactions; H62/H63 screen-quality interactions; H64 CDKN2A adenocarcinoma interaction. New proposals registered: PTEN mean in high screen quality, PTEN mean in high aneuploidy, PTEN mean in TP53-loss, KEAP1 mean in TP53-loss. Next priorities: analyze these conditional means plus H30/H31/H62/H63; if PTEN conditional evidence remains consistent, consider stricter validation; otherwise treat PTEN as exploratory. Keep all claims dataset-specific, non-causal."
